<DetailLayout title="My placement at Metricell" description="A year of software development at Metricell: asset management, telecom mapping and a computer-vision pilot for road inspection.">
  <div class="experience-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Experience / August 2024 — August 2025</p><h1>A year of building<br />at Metricell<span class="terracotta">.</span></h1><p class="case-summary">From learning a new codebase to taking responsibility for a pilot product.</p><p>I joined Metricell as a placement software developer in Horsham. Across telecommunications, asset management and road inspection, I worked with developers, sales colleagues and stakeholders to turn requirements into usable software.</p><ul class="tags"><li>C# / .NET</li><li>TypeScript</li><li>React</li><li>SQL Server</li><li>Mapbox GL JS</li></ul></div><MediaImage src={media.reception} alt="Austin standing beside the Metricell sign in reception." caption="At Metricell during my placement." eager /></div>
  <nav class="detail-nav" aria-label="Placement projects"><a href="#telecom">Telecommunications</a><a href="#assets">Asset management</a><a href="#vision">Computer vision</a><a href="#learning">Lessons & report</a></nav>
  <section id="telecom" class="experience-project"><p class="eyebrow">01 / Telecommunications</p><h2>Understanding networks through software</h2><div class="prose-columns"><div><h3>Purpose & contribution</h3><p>I began with a customer-care tool for a telecommunications operator, working on frontend tasks with my mentor. I later took responsibility for another telecom project’s frontend, adapting the interface and working with network KPI and coverage data.</p><p>My work also included a CloudRF-powered mapping feature in TypeScript and C#, calculating radio-propagation values from user-selected coordinates. The feature supported analysis across more than 100 sites.</p></div><div><h3>Decisions & lessons</h3><p>The work required me to learn C# and TypeScript within an established codebase, understand how SQL data feeds the interface, and write code that other developers could maintain. Working to client deadlines made clear communication and reusable components practical necessities.</p><p>I collaborated with developers and business stakeholders on the customer-care portal, translating requirements into technical changes rather than treating the interface as a separate piece of work.</p></div></div></section>
  <section id="assets" class="experience-project"><p class="eyebrow">02 / Asset management</p><h2>Making asset data useful</h2><div class="prose-columns"><div><h3>The problem</h3><p>A customer needed to view telecommunications assets and track their progress through an acquisition process. Our team revived an existing platform and adapted it to the new requirements.</p><h3>My contribution</h3><p>I acted as technical lead for a team of three and organised the asset data. I built a C# data loader that mapped columns in CSV and Excel files into SQL tables, making updates available to the platform.</p></div><div><h3>Demand Score</h3><p>I integrated Demand Score to help users assess the context around an asset, including population density, schools, hospitals and transport links. Discussions with sales colleagues and the customer shaped how this information was presented.</p><h3>What I learnt</h3><p>The data loader required close collaboration with colleagues on existing import conventions. The customer discussions helped me connect technical choices to the questions a user actually needed to answer.</p></div></div><ImageGallery images={[{src:media.demand,alt:'Map with a coloured grid displaying demand data across an urban area.',caption:'Demand Score visualisation. The placement report places this map in the asset-management case study.'},{src:media.asset,alt:'Asset analysis panel with a map, asset attributes and a street-level image.',caption:'Asset analysis brings location and asset attributes into a single view. This corresponds to the asset-management example in the report.'}]} /></section>
  <section id="vision" class="experience-project"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Computer vision pilot</p><h2>A working view of road defects</h2><div class="prose-columns"><div><h3>Purpose & ownership</h3><p>The pilot explored how road inspectors could review defects detected in road imagery. I inherited the platform from another developer and became its sole full-stack developer, working with the sales team, directors and experienced colleagues.</p><h3>LiveFeed and inspection workflow</h3><p>I developed the React LiveFeed interface so inspectors could review defects and organise them into action lists, including items awaiting repair and completed work. The aim was to make the existing analysis easier to use in an inspection workflow.</p></div><div><h3>Geospatial decisions</h3><p>I adapted an area-based aggregation layer to use postcode polygons, relating road segments and their defects to areas inspectors could recognise. For customer road assets, I normalised different geometries into points for display and used separate tables plus an additional-data field to retain varying attributes.</p><h3>Data loading</h3><p>With my mentor, I built a program using C# and NetTopologySuite, automating the processing of road shapefiles into the format required for the platform. The solution reduced customer platform setup time by over 50%, with test imports completed in under 10 minutes.</p></div></div><ImageGallery images={[{src:media.postcode,alt:'UK map divided into coloured geographic areas for the road-inspection platform.',caption:'Area-based road analysis shown in the computer-vision section of the placement report.'},{src:media.livefeed,alt:'Road-defect interface showing a road image, a pothole classification and associated details.',caption:'The LiveFeed view combines road imagery and defect information for review. The report describes my work on its usability and action-list workflow.'}]} /><p class="evidence-intro">This was a pilot. The materials do not establish a measured improvement in defect-detection accuracy or an operational reduction in inspection costs.</p></section>
  <section id="learning" class="experience-project"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Reflection</p><h2>What stayed with me</h2><div class="prose-columns"><p>I learnt to ask for context early, discuss data structures with the people who would use them, and document work for the next developer. The placement moved me between team delivery and individual ownership, with different expectations in each setting.</p></div><DocumentLinks file="documents/metricell-placement-report.pdf" title="Metricell placement report · public copy" viewLabel="View report" summary="A searchable PDF of the report’s contents, case studies and reflection. The administrative cover pages and signed confidentiality form are omitted from this public copy; the original report remains unchanged." /></section>
</DetailLayout>
